WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:02.460
- [Narrator] More than 23,000 songs

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:05.160
are Shazamed every minute in the world,

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:07.170
but how does it identify
the music around you,

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:10.083
after recording just seconds
of audio from your phone?

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.860
Shazam has to create a
unique audio fingerprint,

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:14.850
of what you're hearing,

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:17.820
and then search its database
for a matching fingerprint.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.890
That means simplifying
sound to a bunch of data,

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.993
that can be processed incredibly fast.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.437
This is "The Tech Behind Shazam."

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.650
The idea behind Shazam is simple,

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:30.270
take the audio captured on your phone,

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:31.890
and convert it into something a computer

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:33.900
can easily compare to other songs.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.780
In acoustics, this is
called audio fingerprinting,

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:39.060
simplifying a song to a unique signature,

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:42.720
that you can identify even
through lots of background noise.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.190
The first step to creating Shazam,

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:46.110
was digitizing a large amount of music,

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.660
since there was no existing
database it could use.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:52.813
- We literally hired about
30, 18 year olds to work,

00:00:52.813 --> 00:00:55.020
eight hours a day in
three eight hours shifts,

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:58.350
so 24 hours a day, actually,
putting CDs into computers,

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:01.080
that ran custom software, that
we built from the ground up.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:05.850
We had 100,000 CDs, which
is about 1.7 million songs.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:07.680
Because CDs don't contain metadata,

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.020
they had to type the name of every song,

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.730
every album title, and every artist,

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:13.890
as they built this massive database.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.750
- [Narrator] Once they
had digitized each song,

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.130
they had to convert them into something

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:20.130
that looks like this, a spectrogram.

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:22.530
Spectrograms map out
frequency content over time.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.480
They also display signal power level,

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.340
related to how loud something sounds,

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.290
which is represented by color.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.450
- In this spectrogram of bird song,

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.610
we have time progressing to the right,

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:34.950
as is always done in spectrograms,

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.050
and then frequency on the vertical axis.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:40.083
So this is coming down. (whistles)

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:45.060
That's not the right frequency
zone, but that's the idea.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.860
- [Narrator] Julius Smith
is an Emeritus Professor,

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.630
at Stanford Center for Computer Research,

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:50.220
in Music and Acoustics.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.470
He was also an early
consultant for Shazam,

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:55.260
and assisted with one of
the company's early patents.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:57.000
With music, spectrograms often look

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.080
more complicated than this,

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:01.200
because you have to deal
with harmonics or overtones,

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.490
which get softer as you move up,

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.320
from the fundamental frequency.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.330
- Beethoven's "Fifth Symphony,"

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:08.910
the opening measure is bum, bum, bum,

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:11.970
and then the next hole measure is bum.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.760
So this is the classic ba-ba-ba-bum,

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:16.170
ba-ba-ba-bum, in score.

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:18.270
And then over here in the spectrogram,

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:20.820
we have the same thing,
not as clearly written,

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:23.762
but clear to hear, and
that is ba-ba-ba-bum.

00:02:23.762 --> 00:02:25.119
("Fifth Symphony" playing)

00:02:25.119 --> 00:02:26.202
Ba-ba-ba-bum.

00:02:28.203 --> 00:02:29.640
- [Narrator] But if Shazam had to match

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:31.980
spectrograms like this, it
would take an incredibly

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:33.900
long time to return a result.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:35.990
The first key insight
was to focus on peaks,

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:38.100
in the spectrogram, because those peaks

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.200
are the main thing the brain processes.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.600
Shazam simplifies a spectrogram like this,

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:44.370
to a scatter plot of peaks.

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:45.570
Many of these peaks make it through

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:47.490
background noise but you may also end up

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:49.650
with a bunch of additional
meaningless peaks.

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:52.530
- So let's say we have a
spectrogram of a piece of music,

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:55.650
and then we look at the
spectrogram and how it changes,

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:57.000
as noise gets brought in.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.220
So let's say people start
walking into the bar,

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:00.600
people start drinking a lot,

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.010
people start making a lot of noise.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.380
Then what you see is the
original spectrogram,

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:07.410
plus a lot of new activity,
and the brain is very good

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:10.200
at understanding superpositions of sounds.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.260
If people can do it, then the
computer can do it, right?

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:14.700
- [Narrator] The core
of Shazams technology

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:15.900
is matching scatter plots,

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.000
that essentially chart
the most powerful signals,

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:19.800
at different frequencies over time.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.900
It also develops some
important processing steps,

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.470
to speed things up so you
can get a result, quickly.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:27.360
Instead of searching
its database for a match

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.400
of all these points in
the proper sequence,

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.260
Shazam looks for multiple points at once,

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:34.290
and doesn't worry about the
overall sequence until the end.

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:36.360
For example, say you
were in a coffee shop,

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.250
playing classical music.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:40.950
You open Shazam and it records your audio.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.133
First, it makes a spectrogram like this.

00:03:43.133 --> 00:03:45.000
Then it isolates the peaks,

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.010
and makes a scatter plot like this.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:49.860
Though in reality, it would
probably have more points.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.740
Shazam connects nearby peaks
to form a bunch of pairs.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:54.810
Then Shazam searches for matching pairs,

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.420
in an organized database
of millions of songs.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:58.380
If there are enough matches,

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.960
in the same song and they're
aligned properly over time,

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:03.180
Shazam can name the tune, in this case,

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:07.080
a specific rendition of
Bach's "Minuet and G Major."

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.060
After about two and a
half years of development,

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:10.890
Shazam launched in 2002,

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:12.750
well before the smartphone era.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:14.160
- Everyone was carrying
these mobile phones around,

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:15.990
everywhere, and really you could only

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:17.340
just do two things with them.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:20.280
You could make phone calls and
you could send text messages

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:22.410
and I guess you could also
play the game, "Snake."

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:24.150
- [Narrator] Here's how
Shazam worked back then.

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:26.550
This is one of its earliest pitch decks.

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:28.800
Initially, Shazam was a
service that people in the UK

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.170
could access by dialing 2580.

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:33.150
After recording 15 seconds of audio,

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:34.140
the call would disconnect,

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.720
and the user would get a text
with the name of the song.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.150
While the fundamental
algorithm has stayed the same,

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:40.890
engineers have made some modifications,

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:42.720
to improve its recognition rate.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:44.190
- It was good enough that
it felt like it worked

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:45.900
most of the time, and it
really delighted people,

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:48.720
that it would work in these
like bars and clubs and so on.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:51.000
We also had to then add
additional tweaks to deal

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.060
with the fact that
music in the real world,

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:57.870
also gets adjusted in terms
of speed, tempo, and pitch.

00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:00.900
So there are, for example,
DJs might adjust the speed,

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.000
of music when they're
playing vinyl records.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:04.890
- [Narrator] Shazam
wasn't an immediate hit.

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:07.440
It had to sell some of its
patents to fund development,

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.210
though it later bought them back,

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:12.510
And it wasn't profitable,
it says, until 2016.

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:15.150
Barton was CEO for four
years before stepping down,

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:17.700
though he continued to serve
on the company's board.

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:19.680
- When I left Shazam, it was running

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.380
through a long period of barely surviving.

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:26.790
Shazam was a story of nearly failing,

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:28.350
nearly going bankrupt,

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:31.110
incredible resilience over many years.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:32.700
It didn't really hit a hockey stick,

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:34.800
of incredible user adoption,

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:36.990
until the app store launched in 2008.

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:39.630
- [Narrator] Eventually,
Apple bought Shazam in 2018.

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:41.250
It's now one of the most popular

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:43.260
free music apps in the app store.

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:45.690
- Once you're paying for
unlimited streaming of music,

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:47.430
what is it you're
actually gonna listen to?

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:50.340
Because you have access to
everything as much as you want,

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:53.340
and Shazam solves one form of discovery.

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:54.869
You're not only discovering music,

00:05:54.869 --> 00:05:57.270
when your friend tells
you about a great band,

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:00.270
but you're actually discovering
it in your daily life.

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:01.590
- [Narrator] The technology behind Shazam,

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:05.190
has grown significantly since
Apple's acquisition in 2018.

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:07.200
When it bought Shazam,
it also gave the company

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:09.630
access to Apple Music's large library.

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:12.540
Today, Shazam can identify most
of the Apple Music catalog,

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:14.790
of over 100 million songs.

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:19.020
- We created an app many years
before apps actually existed.

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:22.440
Part of the art of a startup
is the timing aspect,

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:25.290
and so fortunately we
found a way to survive,

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:27.070
through those tough years.

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:29.737
(upbeat music)

